Refugees",,United Kingdom,"We assess labour providers and subcontractors through pre-qualification and enhanced due diligence. Identity, right-to-work and employment checks are required, alongside site inductions, worker welfare monitoring, confidential reporting routes and regular supply chain reviews. Project-specific Modern Slavery Management Plans identify vulnerable workers and set appropriate controls.","Workers producing polysilicon for photovoltaic panels in Xinjiang, China, where there is a heightened risk of forced labour.",Within your supply chains,Tier 3 suppliers and below,Other vulnerable groups,Uyghur and other ethnic minority,China,"We assess PV panel suppliers through enhanced due diligence, requiring evidence on product origin, polysilicon sourcing and supply chain traceability. Suppliers linked to higher-risk regions are subject to further review and recorded on our Modern Slavery High-Risk Supplier Register. Where sufficient assurance cannot be provided, we seek alternative products or suppliers and continue to monitor emerging risks.",,,,,,,,,Yes,,,"Over the next 12 months, we will measure progress through project-specific Modern Slavery Management Plans, periodic reviews of labour providers and higher-risk suppliers, improved evidence of lower-tier supply chain due diligence, increased training and refresher briefings, and reviews of multilingual and anonymous reporting routes. Health and safety inspections will check welfare, inductions and site information, with findings and actions reported to senior management.",2026-08-17T09:27:14,https://downloads.modern-slavery-statement-registry.service.gov.uk/pdf-published/rjEEdAeY/2026/NCL POL 024 Modern Slavery and Human Trafficking Statement.pdf
